  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 120 AM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nLatest operational and ensemble model data continues to support \nupper level ridging build back across the Southern Rockies through \nthe middle of the week and looks to centered across the Four Corners \nregion by the end of the work week. This will bring in another slug \nof monsoonal moisture within modest west to southwest flow aloft, \nwith ensemble data supporting PWATs of 150 to 175 percent of normal  \nacross the region through Thursday. The abundant moisture, combined\nwith strong summer solar insolation, and a few minor disturbances \ntranslating through the flow will keep scattered to numerous afternoon\nand evening showers and thunderstorms in the forecast over and near\nthe higher terrain through Thursday, with increasing daily chances\nof storms across the plains, peaking on Wednesday, as said progged \ndisturbance translates across the Rockies. With modest flow aloft,\nstorms will be moving, however, with the amount of moisture available,\nstorms will be capable of producing very heavy rainfall in a short \namount of time, leading to the threat of flash flooding. We will need \nto monitor daily conditions and forecast through the upcoming week\nfor the potential inclusion of Flash Flood Watches, especially for \nthe Aspen Acres burn area. With cooling aloft and the expected increase\nin convection, we will see temperatures cooling back to at and slightly\nabove seasonal levels through the work week.   \n\nLonger term model and ensemble data continue to support drier air \nworking back into the Rockies for the end of the week into the weekend \nand early next week, as the upper ridge builds back into the Desert \nSouthwest and Great Basin region, bringing more north to northwest \nflow aloft into the Rockies. There will likely be enough residual \nmoisture to support scattered afternoon showers and storms over the \nhigher terrain through the weekend, though storms will likely be on \nthe drier side and less efficient rain makers. Temperatures will start\nto warm back to above seasonal levels into early next week, as the \nridge starts to build back into the Rockies.",
